delimiter $ ( $ $ ) $;

provable sort wff;

--| (_Note_: This inference rule and the next one, ~ a1ii , will normally never appear in a completed proof. They can be ignored if you are using this database to assist learning logic; please start with the statement ~ wn instead.) This inference says "if ` ph ` is true then ` ph ` is true". This inference requires no axioms for its proof, and is useful as a copy-paste mechanism during proof development in mmj2. It is normally not referenced in the final version of a proof, since it is always redundant. You can remove this using the metamath-exe (Metamath program) Proof Assistant using the "MM-PA> MINIMIZE__WITH *" command. This is the inference associated with ~ id , hence its name. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem idi (ph: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| (_Note_: This inference rule and the previous one, ~ idi , will normally never appear in a completed proof.) This is a technical inference to assist proof development. It provides a temporary way to add an independent subproof to a proof under development, for later assignment to a normal proof step. The Metamath (Metamath-exe) program Proof Assistant requires proofs to be developed backwards from the conclusion with no gaps, and it has no mechanism that lets the user work on isolated subproofs. This inference provides a workaround for this limitation. It can be inserted at any point in a proof to allow an independent subproof to be developed on the side, for later use as part of the final proof. _Instructions_: <HTML><ol><li>Assign this inference to any unknown step in the proof. Typically, the last unknown step is the most convenient, since <code>MM-PA&gt; ASSIGN LAST</code> can be used. This step will be replicated in hypothesis a1ii.1, from where the development of the main proof can continue.</li><li>Develop the independent subproof backwards from hypothesis a1ii.2. If desired, use a <code>MM-PA&gt; LET STEP</code> command to pre-assign the conclusion of the independent subproof to a1ii.2.</li><li>After the independent subproof is complete, use <code>MM-PA&gt; IMPROVE ALL</code> to assign it automatically to an unknown step in the main proof that matches it.</li><li>After the entire proof is complete, use <code>MM-PA> MINIMIZE_WITH *</code> to clean up (discard) all ~ a1ii references automatically.</ol></HTML> This can also be used to apply subproofs from other theorems. In step 2, simply assign the theorem to a1ii.2, and run <HTML><code>MM-PA&gt; EXPAND &lt;theorem&gt;</code></HTML> to "import" a subproof from another theorem. This inference was originally designed to assist importing partially completed Proof Worksheets from the mmj2 Proof Assistant GUI, but it can also be useful on its own. Interestingly, no axioms are required for its proof. It is the inference associated with ~ a1i . (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-2006.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem a1ii (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ ph $;

--| If ` ph ` is a wff, so is ` -. ph ` or "not ` ph ` ". Part of the recursive definition of a wff (well-formed formula). In classical logic (which is our logic), a wff is interpreted as either true or false. So if ` ph ` is true, then ` -. ph ` is false; if ` ph ` is false, then ` -. ph ` is true. Traditionally, Greek letters are used to represent wffs, and we follow this convention. In propositional calculus, we define only wffs built up from other wffs, i.e. there is no starting or "atomic" wff. Later, in predicate calculus, we will extend the basic wff definition by including atomic wffs ( ~ weq and ~ wel ).
term wn (ph: wff): wff;

--| If ` ph ` and ` ps ` are wff's, so is ` ( ph -> ps ) ` or " ` ph ` implies ` ps ` ". Part of the recursive definition of a wff. The resulting wff is (interpreted as) false when ` ph ` is true and ` ps ` is false; it is true otherwise. Think of the truth table for an OR gate with input ` ph ` connected through an inverter. After we state the axioms of propositional calculus ( ~ ax-1 , ~ ax-2 , ~ ax-3 , and ~ ax-mp ) and define the biconditional ( ~ df-bi ), the constant true ` T. ` ( ~ df-tru ), and the constant false ` F. ` ( ~ df-fal ), we will be able to prove these truth table values: ` ( ( T. -> T. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ truimtru ), ` ( ( T. -> F. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ truimfal ), ` ( ( F. -> T. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ falimtru ), and ` ( ( F. -> F. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ falimfal ). These have straightforward meanings, for example, ` ( ( T. -> T. ) <-> T. ) ` just means "the value of ` ( T. -> T. ) ` is ` T. ` ". The left-hand wff is called the antecedent, and the right-hand wff is called the consequent. In the case of ` ( ph -> ( ps -> ch ) ) ` , the middle ` ps ` may be informally called either an antecedent or part of the consequent depending on context. Contrast with ` <-> ` ( ~ df-bi ), ` /\ ` ( ~ df-an ), and ` \/ ` ( ~ df-or ). This is called "material implication" and the arrow is usually read as "implies". However, material implication is not identical to the meaning of "implies" in natural language. For example, the word "implies" may suggest a causal relationship in natural language. Material implication does not require any causal relationship. Also, note that in material implication, if the consequent is true then the wff is always true (even if the antecedent is false). Thus, if "implies" means material implication, it is true that "if the moon is made of green cheese that implies that 5=5" (because 5=5). Similarly, if the antecedent is false, the wff is always true. Thus, it is true that, "if the moon is made of green cheese that implies that 5=7" (because the moon is not actually made of green cheese). A contradiction implies anything ( ~ pm2.21i ). In short, material implication has a very specific technical definition, and misunderstandings of it are sometimes called "paradoxes of logical implication".
term wi (ph ps: wff): wff;

--| Rule of Modus Ponens. The postulated inference rule of propositional calculus. See, e.g., Rule 1 of [Hamilton] p. 73. The rule says, "if ` ph ` is true, and ` ph ` implies ` ps ` , then ` ps ` must also be true". This rule is sometimes called "detachment", since it detaches the minor premise from the major premise. "Modus ponens" is short for "modus ponendo ponens", a Latin phrase that means "the mode that by affirming affirms" - remark in [Sanford] p. 39. This rule is similar to the rule of modus tollens ~ mto . Note: In some web page displays such as the Statement List, the symbols " ` & ` " and " ` => ` " informally indicate the relationship between the hypotheses and the assertion (conclusion), abbreviating the English words "and" and "implies". They are not part of the formal language. (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-1992.)
axiom ax_mp (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Axiom _Simp_. Axiom A1 of [Margaris] p. 49. One of the 3 axioms of propositional calculus. The 3 axioms are also given as Definition 2.1 of [Hamilton] p. 28. This axiom is called _Simp_ or "the principle of simplification" in _Principia Mathematica_ (Theorem *2.02 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 100) because "it enables us to pass from the joint assertion of ` ph ` and ` ps ` to the assertion of ` ph ` simply". It is Proposition 1 of [Frege1879] p. 26, its first axiom. (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-1992.)
axiom ax_1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ph) $;

--| Axiom _Frege_. Axiom A2 of [Margaris] p. 49. One of the 3 axioms of propositional calculus. It "distributes" an antecedent over two consequents. This axiom was part of Frege's original system and is known as _Frege_ in the literature; see Proposition 2 of [Frege1879] p. 26. It is also proved as Theorem *2.77 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. The other direction of this axiom also turns out to be true, as demonstrated by ~ pm5.41 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-1992.)
axiom ax_2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Axiom _Transp_. Axiom A3 of [Margaris] p. 49. One of the 3 axioms of propositional calculus. It swaps or "transposes" the order of the consequents when negation is removed. An informal example is that the statement "if there are no clouds in the sky, it is not raining" implies the statement "if it is raining, there are clouds in the sky". This axiom is called _Transp_ or "the principle of transposition" in _Principia Mathematica_ (Theorem *2.17 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 103). We will also use the term "contraposition" for this principle, although the reader is advised that in the field of philosophical logic, "contraposition" has a different technical meaning. (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-1992.) Use its alias ~ con4 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wi ps ph) $;

--| A double modus ponens inference. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-1994.)
theorem mp2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ ch $;

--| A double modus ponens inference. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jan-2013.)
theorem mp2b (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ ch $;

--| Inference introducing an antecedent. Inference associated with ~ ax-1 . Its associated inference is ~ a1ii . See ~ conventions for a definition of "associated inference". (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.)
theorem a1i (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ps ph $;

--| Inference introducing two antecedents. Two applications of ~ a1i . Inference associated with ~ 2a1 . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Aug-2009.)
theorem _2a1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch ph) $;

--| Inference detaching an antecedent and introducing a new one. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem mp1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch ps $;

--| Inference distributing an antecedent. Inference associated with ~ ax-2 . Its associated inference is ~ mpd . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.)
theorem a2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch) $;

--| A modus ponens deduction. A translation of natural deduction rule ` -> ` E ( ` -> ` elimination), see ~ natded . Deduction form of ~ ax-mp . Inference associated with ~ a2i . Commuted form of ~ mpcom . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.)
theorem mpd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Inference adding common antecedents in an implication. Inference associated with ~ imim2 . Its associated inference is ~ syl . (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-1992.)
theorem imim2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wi ch ph) (wi ch ps) $;

--| An inference version of the transitive laws for implication ~ imim2 and ~ imim1 (and ~ imim1i and ~ imim2i ), which Russell and Whitehead call "the principle of the syllogism ... because ... the syllogism in Barbara is derived from [[ ~ syl ]" (quote after Theorem *2.06 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 101). Some authors call this law a "hypothetical syllogism". Its associated inference is ~ mp2b . (A bit of trivia: this is the most commonly referenced assertion in our database (13449 times as of 22-Jul-2021). In second place is ~ eqid (9597 times), followed by ~ adantr (8861 times), ~ syl2anc (7421 times), ~ adantl (6403 times), and ~ simpr (5829 times). The Metamath program command 'show usage' shows the number of references.) (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-1992.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 20-Oct-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Jul-2012.)
theorem syl (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Inference chaining two syllogisms ~ syl . Inference associated with ~ imim12i . (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-1992.)
theorem _3syl (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Inference chaining three syllogisms ~ syl . (Contributed by BJ, 14-Jul-2018.) The use of this theorem is marked "discouraged" because it can cause the Metamath program "MM-PA> MINIMIZE__WITH *" command to have very long run times. However, feel free to use "MM-PA> MINIMIZE__WITH 4syl / OVERRIDE" if you wish. Remember to update the "discouraged" file if it gets used. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _4syl (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi th ta $ >
  $ wi ph ta $;

--| A nested modus ponens inference. Inference associated with ~ com12 . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Stefan Allan, 20-Mar-2006.)
theorem mpi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| A syllogism combined with a modus ponens inference. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 25-Jul-2011.)
theorem mpisyl (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Principle of identity. Theorem *2.08 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 101. For another version of the proof directly from axioms, see ~ idALT . Its associated inference, ~ idi , requires no axioms for its proof, contrary to ~ id . Note that the second occurrences of ` ph ` in Steps 1 and 2 may be simultaneously replaced by any wff ` ps ` , which may ease the understanding of the proof. (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Stefan Allan, 20-Mar-2006.)
theorem id (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph ph $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ id . This version is proved directly from the axioms for demonstration purposes. This proof is a popular example in the literature and is identical, step for step, to the proofs of Theorem 1 of [Margaris] p. 51, Example 2.7(a) of [Hamilton] p. 31, Lemma 10.3 of [BellMachover] p. 36, and Lemma 1.8 of [Mendelson] p. 36. It is also "Our first proof" in Hirst and Hirst's _A Primer for Logic and Proof_ p. 17 (PDF p. 23) at ~ http://www.appstate.edu/~~hirstjl/primer/hirst.pdf . Note that the second occurrences of ` ph ` in Steps 1 to 4 and the sixth in Step 3 may be simultaneously replaced by any wff ` ps ` , which may ease the understanding of the proof. For a shorter version of the proof that takes advantage of previously proved theorems, see ~ id . (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-1992.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Use ~ id instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem idALT (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph ph $;

--| Principle of identity ~ id with antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 26-Nov-1995.)
theorem idd (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ps) $;

--| Deduction introducing an embedded antecedent. Deduction form of ~ ax-1 and ~ a1i . (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Stefan Allan, 20-Mar-2006.)
theorem a1d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch ps) $;

--| Deduction introducing two antecedents. Two applications of ~ a1d . Deduction associated with ~ 2a1 and ~ 2a1i . (Contributed by BJ, 10-Aug-2020.)
theorem _2a1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wi th ps)) $;

--| Add two antecedents to a wff. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Aug-2009.)
theorem a1i13 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ps th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $;

--| A double form of ~ ax-1 . Its associated inference is ~ 2a1i . Its associated deduction is ~ 2a1d . (Contributed by BJ, 10-Aug-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Sep-2020.)
theorem _2a1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ph)) $;

--| Deduction distributing an embedded antecedent. Deduction form of ~ ax-2 . (Contributed by NM, 23-Jun-1994.)
theorem a2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ps th)) $;

--| Syllogism inference with commutation of antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 29-Aug-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 2-Feb-2006.) (Proof shortened by Stefan Allan, 23-Feb-2006.)
theorem sylcom (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| Syllogism inference with commuted antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 24-May-2005.)
theorem syl5com (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $;

--| Inference that swaps (commutes) antecedents in an implication. Inference associated with ~ pm2.04 . Its associated inference is ~ mpi . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Aug-2012.)
theorem com12 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ph ch) $;

--| A syllogism inference. Commuted form of an instance of ~ syl . (Contributed by BJ, 25-Oct-2021.)
theorem syl11 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th ph $ >
  $ wi ps (wi th ch) $;

--| A syllogism rule of inference. The first premise is used to replace the second antecedent of the second premise. (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-May-2013.)
theorem syl5 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph th) $;

--| A syllogism rule of inference. The second premise is used to replace the consequent of the first premise. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Jul-2012.)
theorem syl6 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| Combine ~ syl5 and ~ syl6 . (Contributed by NM, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem syl56 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi th ta $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph ta) $;

--| Syllogism inference with commuted antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 25-May-2005.)
theorem syl6com (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ph th) $;

--| Modus ponens inference with commutation of antecedents. Commuted form of ~ mpd . (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-1996.)
theorem mpcom (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ps ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ps ch $;

--| Syllogism inference with common nested antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2004.)
theorem syli (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ps (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph th) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ph th) $;

--| Replace two antecedents. Implication-only version of ~ syl2an . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 14-May-2013.)
theorem syl2im (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi ps (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch ta) $;

--| A commuted version of ~ syl2im . Implication-only version of ~ syl2anr . (Contributed by BJ, 20-Oct-2021.)
theorem syl2imc (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi ps (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph ta) $;

--| This theorem, sometimes called "Assertion" or "Pon" (for "ponens"), can be thought of as a closed form of modus ponens ~ ax-mp . Theorem *2.27 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 104. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-1993.)
theorem pm2_27 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ph ps) ps) $;

--| A nested modus ponens deduction. Double deduction associated with ~ ax-mp . Deduction associated with ~ mpd . (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.)
theorem mpdd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A nested modus ponens deduction. Deduction associated with ~ mpi . (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2004.)
theorem mpid (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A nested modus ponens deduction. (Contributed by NM, 16-Apr-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 15-Jan-2008.)
theorem mpdi (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A doubly nested modus ponens inference. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 31-Jul-2012.)
theorem mpii (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| Syllogism deduction. Deduction associated with ~ syl . See ~ conventions for the meaning of "associated deduction" or "deduction form". (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 19-Feb-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Aug-2012.)
theorem syld (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| Syllogism deduction. Commuted form of ~ syld . (Contributed by BJ, 25-Oct-2021.)
theorem syldc (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ph th) $;

--| A double modus ponens deduction. Deduction associated with ~ mp2 . (Contributed by NM, 23-May-2013.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Jul-2013.)
theorem mp2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Double deduction introducing an antecedent. Deduction associated with ~ a1d . Double deduction associated with ~ ax-1 and ~ a1i . (Contributed by NM, 17-Dec-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 15-Jan-2008.)
theorem a1dd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi th ch)) $;

--| Double deduction introducing two antecedents. Two applications of ~ 2a1dd . Deduction associated with ~ 2a1d . Double deduction associated with ~ 2a1 and ~ 2a1i . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Aug-2009.)
theorem _2a1dd (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi th (wi ta ch))) $;

--| Inference absorbing redundant antecedent. Inference associated with ~ pm2.43 . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 28-Nov-2008.)
theorem pm2_43i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Deduction absorbing redundant antecedent. Deduction associated with ~ pm2.43 and ~ pm2.43i . (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 28-Nov-2008.)
theorem pm2_43d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $;

--| Inference absorbing redundant antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-1995.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 28-Nov-2008.)
theorem pm2_43a (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ps (wi ph (wi ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ph ch) $;

--| Inference absorbing redundant antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 31-Oct-1995.)
theorem pm2_43b (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ps (wi ph (wi ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $;

--| Absorption of redundant antecedent. Also called the "Contraction" or "Hilbert" axiom. Theorem *2.43 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 106. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 15-Aug-2004.)
theorem pm2_43 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ph ps)) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Deduction adding nested antecedents. Deduction associated with ~ imim2 and ~ imim2i . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.)
theorem imim2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wi th ps) (wi th ch)) $;

--| A closed form of syllogism (see ~ syl ). Theorem *2.05 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 100. Its associated inference is ~ imim2i . Its associated deduction is ~ imim2d . An alternate proof from more basic results is given by ~ ax-1 followed by ~ a2d . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Sep-2012.)
theorem imim2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ch ph) (wi ch ps)) $;

--| Deduction embedding an antecedent. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 4-Oct-2013.)
theorem embantd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ps ch) th) $;

--| Triple syllogism deduction. Deduction associated with ~ 3syld . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Aug-2009.)
theorem _3syld (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ta) $;

--| A double syllogism inference. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 20-Apr-2011.)
theorem sylsyld (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch ta) $;

--| Inference joining two implications. Inference associated with ~ imim12 . Its associated inference is ~ 3syl . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 29-Oct-2011.)
theorem imim12i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph th) $;

--| Inference adding common consequents in an implication, thereby interchanging the original antecedent and consequent. Inference associated with ~ imim1 . Its associated inference is ~ syl . (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Aug-2012.)
theorem imim1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch) $;

--| Inference adding three nested antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2006.)
theorem imim3i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wi th ph) (wi (wi th ps) (wi th ch)) $;

--| A syllogism inference combined with contraction. (Contributed by NM, 4-May-1994.) (Revised by NM, 13-Jul-2013.)
theorem sylc (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| A syllogism inference combined with contraction. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 7-Jul-2011.)
theorem syl3c (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta)) $ >
  $ wi ph ta $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 8-Jul-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Sep-2012.)
theorem syl6mpi (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ th $ >
  $ wi ch (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ta) $;

--| Modus ponens combined with a syllogism inference. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 20-Apr-2011.)
theorem mpsyl (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ps th $;

--| Modus ponens combined with a double syllogism inference. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 22-Jul-2012.)
theorem mpsylsyld (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch ta) $;

--| Inference combining ~ syl6 with contraction. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 2-May-2011.)
theorem syl6c (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ta) $;

--| A syllogism inference combined with contraction. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 18-Mar-2012.)
theorem syl6ci (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ch (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ta) $;

--| Nested syllogism deduction. Deduction associated with ~ syld . Double deduction associated with ~ syl . (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-May-2013.)
theorem syldd (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi th ta)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ta)) $;

--| A nested syllogism deduction. Deduction associated with ~ syl5 . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Josh Purinton, 29-Dec-2000.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 2-Feb-2006.)
theorem syl5d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th (wi ch ta)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th (wi ps ta)) $;

--| A syllogism rule of inference. The first premise is used to replace the third antecedent of the second premise. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Aug-2012.)
theorem syl7 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wi th (wi ps ta)) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi th (wi ph ta)) $;

--| A nested syllogism deduction. Deduction associated with ~ syl6 . (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Josh Purinton, 29-Dec-2000.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 2-Feb-2006.)
theorem syl6d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ta)) $;

--| A syllogism rule of inference. The second premise is used to replace the consequent of the first premise. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Aug-2012.)
theorem syl8 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi th ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ta)) $;

--| A nested syllogism inference with different antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Josh Purinton, 29-Dec-2000.)
theorem syl9 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ch ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th (wi ps ta)) $;

--| A nested syllogism inference with different antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem syl9r (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ch ta) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ph (wi ps ta)) $;

--| A nested syllogism inference. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 17-Jul-2011.)
theorem syl10 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi th ta)) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ta et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi th et)) $;

--| Triple deduction introducing an antecedent to a wff. Deduction associated with ~ a1dd . Double deduction associated with ~ a1d . Triple deduction associated with ~ ax-1 and ~ a1i . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Aug-2009.)
theorem a1ddd (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ta)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $;

--| Deduction combining antecedents and consequents. Deduction associated with ~ imim12 and ~ imim12i . (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 30-Oct-2011.)
theorem imim12d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ch th) (wi ps ta)) $;

--| Deduction adding nested consequents. Deduction associated with ~ imim1 and ~ imim1i . (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Sep-2012.)
theorem imim1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ch th) (wi ps th)) $;

--| A closed form of syllogism (see ~ syl ). Theorem *2.06 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 100. Its associated inference is ~ imim1i . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-May-2013.)
theorem imim1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *2.83 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. Closed form of ~ syld . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_83 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi ph (wi ch th)) (wi ph (wi ps th))) $;

--| Over minimal implicational calculus, Peirce's axiom ~ peirce implies an axiom sometimes called "Roll", ` ( ( ( ph -> ps ) -> ch ) -> ( ( ch -> ph ) -> ph ) ) ` , of which ~ looinv is a special instance. The converse also holds: substitute ` ( ph -> ps ) ` for ` ch ` in Roll and use ~ id and ~ ax-mp . (Contributed by BJ, 15-Jun-2021.)
theorem peirceroll (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ph) ph) (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi (wi ch ph) ph)) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Swap 2nd and 3rd. Deduction associated with ~ com12 . (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Aug-2012.)
theorem com23 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wi ps th)) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Rotate right. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.)
theorem com3r (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph (wi ps th)) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Swap 1st and 3rd. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jul-2012.)
theorem com13 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ps (wi ph th)) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Rotate left. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jul-2012.)
theorem com3l (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch (wi ph th)) $;

--| Swap antecedents. Theorem *2.04 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 100. This was the third axiom in Frege's logic system, specifically Proposition 8 of [Frege1879] p. 35. Its associated inference is ~ com12 . (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Sep-2012.)
theorem pm2_04 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ps (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Swap 3rd and 4th. Deduction associated with ~ com23 . Double deduction associated with ~ com12 . (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.)
theorem com34 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi th (wi ch ta))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Rotate left. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 15-Aug-2004.)
theorem com4l (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ph ta))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Rotate twice. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.)
theorem com4t (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi th (wi ph (wi ps ta))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Rotate right. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.)
theorem com4r (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ta))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Swap 2nd and 4th. Deduction associated with ~ com13 . (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jul-2012.)
theorem com24 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th (wi ch (wi ps ta))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Swap 1st and 4th. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jul-2012.)
theorem com14 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ps (wi ch (wi ph ta))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Swap 4th and 5th. Deduction associated with ~ com34 . Double deduction associated with ~ com23 . Triple deduction associated with ~ com12 . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jun-2009.)
theorem com45 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi ta (wi th et)))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Swap 3rd and 5th. Deduction associated with ~ com24 . Double deduction associated with ~ com13 . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jun-2009.)
theorem com35 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ta (wi th (wi ch et)))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Swap 2nd and 5th. Deduction associated with ~ com14 . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jun-2009.)
theorem com25 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ta (wi ch (wi th (wi ps et)))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Rotate left. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jun-2009.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Jul-2012.)
theorem com5l (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta (wi ph et)))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Swap 1st and 5th. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jun-2009.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Jul-2012.)
theorem com15 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi ta (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ph et)))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Rotate left twice. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jun-2009.)
theorem com52l (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi th (wi ta (wi ph (wi ps et)))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Rotate right twice. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jun-2009.)
theorem com52r (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ta (wi ph (wi ps (wi ch et)))) $;

--| Commutation of antecedents. Rotate right. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 29-Jul-2012.)
theorem com5r (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi ta (wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th et)))) $;

--| Closed form of ~ imim12i and of ~ 3syl . (Contributed by BJ, 16-Jul-2019.)
theorem imim12 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ch th) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph th))) $;

--| Elimination of a nested antecedent. Sometimes called "Syll-Simp" since it is a syllogism applied to ~ ax-1 ("Simplification"). (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 9-May-2013.)
theorem jarr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi ps ch) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ jarr . Partial converse of ~ ja (the other partial converse being ~ jarli ). (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 20-Sep-2013.)
theorem jarri (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ps ch $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ pm2.86 . (Contributed by NM, 29-Jun-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Apr-2013.)
theorem pm2_86d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ps th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $;

--| Converse of Axiom ~ ax-2 . Theorem *2.86 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Apr-2013.)
theorem pm2_86 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) (wi ph (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ pm2.86 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Apr-2013.)
theorem pm2_86i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $;

--| The Linearity Axiom of the infinite-valued sentential logic (L-infinity) of Lukasiewicz. See ~ loowoz for an alternate axiom. (Contributed by Mel L. O'Cat, 12-Aug-2004.)
theorem loolin (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ps ph)) (wi ps ph) $;

--| An alternate for the Linearity Axiom of the infinite-valued sentential logic (L-infinity) of Lukasiewicz ~ loolin , due to Barbara Wozniakowska, _Reports on Mathematical Logic_ 10, 129-137 (1978). (Contributed by Mel L. O'Cat, 8-Aug-2004.)
theorem loowoz (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) (wi (wi ps ph) (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-3 to be used instead of it for labeling consistency. Its associated inference is ~ con4i and its associated deduction is ~ con4d . (Contributed by BJ, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem con4 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wi ps ph) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ con4 . Its associated inference is ~ mt4 . Remark: this can also be proved using ~ notnot followed by ~ nsyl2 , giving a shorter proof but depending on more axioms (namely, ~ ax-1 and ~ ax-2 ). (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.)
theorem con4i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ps ph $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ con4 . (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-1995.)
theorem con4d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch ps) $;

--| The rule of modus tollens. Inference associated with ~ con4i . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 12-May-2013.)
theorem mt4 (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wn ph) $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Modus tollens deduction. Deduction form of ~ mt4 . (Contributed by NM, 9-Jun-2006.)
theorem mt4d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ch) (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Modus tollens inference. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 12-May-2013.)
theorem mt4i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| A contradiction implies anything. Inference associated with ~ pm2.21 . Its associated inference is ~ pm2.24ii . (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1993.)
theorem pm2_21i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| A contradiction implies anything. Inference associated with ~ pm2.21i and ~ pm2.24i . (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2008.)
theorem pm2_24ii (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| A contradiction implies anything. Deduction associated with ~ pm2.21 . (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1996.)
theorem pm2_21d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ pm2.21dd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem pm2_21ddALT (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| From a wff and its negation, anything follows. Theorem *2.21 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 104. Also called the Duns Scotus law. Its commuted form is ~ pm2.24 and its associated inference is ~ pm2.21i . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Sep-2012.)
theorem pm2_21 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Theorem *2.24 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 104. Its associated inference is ~ pm2.24i . Commuted form of ~ pm2.21 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_24 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ph) ps) $;

--| Elimination of a nested antecedent. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 10-May-2013.)
theorem jarl (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi (wn ph) ch) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ jarl . Partial converse of ~ ja (the other partial converse being ~ jarri ). (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 4-Oct-2013.)
theorem jarli (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ch $;

--| Deduction form of the Clavius law ~ pm2.18 . (Contributed by FL, 12-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.) Revised to shorten ~ pm2.18 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 17-Nov-2023.)
theorem pm2_18d (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Clavius law, or "consequentia mirabilis" ("admirable consequence"). If a formula is implied by its negation, then it is true. Can be used in proofs by contradiction. Theorem *2.18 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 103. See also the weak Clavius law ~ pm2.01 . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Nov-2023.)
theorem pm2_18 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) ph) ph $;

--| Inference associated with the Clavius law ~ pm2.18 . (Contributed by BJ, 30-Mar-2020.)
theorem pm2_18i (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Double negation elimination. Converse of ~ notnot and one implication of ~ notnotb . Theorem *2.14 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 102. This was the fifth axiom of Frege, specifically Proposition 31 of [Frege1879] p. 44. In classical logic (our logic) this is always true. In intuitionistic logic this is not always true, and formulas for which it is true are called "stable". (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by David Harvey, 5-Sep-1999.) (Proof shortened by Josh Purinton, 29-Dec-2000.)
theorem notnotr (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wn ph)) ph $;

--| Inference associated with ~ notnotr . For a shorter proof using ~ ax-2 , see ~ notnotriALT . (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Jul-2021.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-2 . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 27-Dec-2022.)
theorem notnotri (ph: wff):
  $ wn (wn ph) $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ notnotri . The proof via ~ notnotr and ~ ax-mp also has three essential steps, but has a total number of steps equal to 8, instead of the present 7, because it has to construct the formula ` ph ` twice and the formula ` -. -. ph ` once, whereas the present proof has to construct the formula ` ph ` twice and the formula ` -. ph ` once, and therefore makes only one use of ~ wn instead of two. This can be checked by running the Metamath command "MM> SHOW PROOF notnotri / NORMAL". (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Jul-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem notnotriALT (ph: wff):
  $ wn (wn ph) $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ notnotr and ~ notnotri . Double negation elimination rule. A translation of the natural deduction rule ` -. -. ` C , ` _G |- -. -. ps => _G |- ps ` ; see ~ natded . This is Definition NNC in [Pfenning] p. 17. This rule is valid in classical logic (our logic), but not in intuitionistic logic. (Contributed by DAW, 8-Feb-2017.)
theorem notnotrd (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| A contraposition deduction. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.)
theorem con2d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wn ps)) $;

--| Contraposition. Theorem *2.03 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 100. (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Feb-2013.)
theorem con2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wn ps)) (wi ps (wn ph)) $;

--| Modus tollens deduction. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jul-1994.)
theorem mt2d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Modus tollens inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Sep-2012.)
theorem mt2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| A negated syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 1-Dec-1995.)
theorem nsyl3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ch ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wn ph) $;

--| A contraposition inference. Its associated inference is ~ mt2 . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 28-Nov-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Jun-2013.)
theorem con2i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ps (wn ph) $;

--| A negated syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Mar-2013.)
theorem nsyl (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ch ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| A negated syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jun-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Nov-2023.)
theorem nsyl2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn ch) ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Double negation introduction. Converse of ~ notnotr and one implication of ~ notnotb . Theorem *2.12 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 101. This was the sixth axiom of Frege, specifically Proposition 41 of [Frege1879] p. 47. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Mar-2013.)
theorem notnot (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn (wn ph)) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ notnot . (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2008.)
theorem notnoti (ph: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wn (wn ph) $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ notnot and ~ notnoti . (Contributed by Jarvin Udandy, 2-Sep-2016.) Avoid biconditional. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 27-Mar-2021.)
theorem notnotd (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wn ps)) $;

--| A contraposition deduction. (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-1992.)
theorem con1d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ch) ps) $;

--| Contraposition. Theorem *2.15 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 102. Its associated inference is ~ con1i . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Feb-2013.)
theorem con1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) ps) (wi (wn ps) ph) $;

--| A contraposition inference. Inference associated with ~ con1 . Its associated inference is ~ mt3 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 28-Nov-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jun-2013.)
theorem con1i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) ph $;

--| Modus tollens deduction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-1995.)
theorem mt3d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Modus tollens inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Sep-2012.)
theorem mt3i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wn ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Inference associated with ~ pm2.24 . Its associated inference is ~ pm2.24ii . (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2001.)
theorem pm2_24i (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ps $;

--| Deduction form of ~ pm2.24 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jan-2006.)
theorem pm2_24d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) ch) $;

--| A contraposition deduction. Deduction form of ~ con3 . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.)
theorem con3d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ch) (wn ps)) $;

--| Contraposition. Theorem *2.16 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 103. This was the fourth axiom of Frege, specifically Proposition 28 of [Frege1879] p. 43. Its associated inference is ~ con3i . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Feb-2013.)
theorem con3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wn ps) (wn ph)) $;

--| A contraposition inference. Inference associated with ~ con3 . Its associated inference is ~ mto . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jun-2013.)
theorem con3i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wn ph) $;

--| Rotate through consequent right. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem con3rr3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wn ch) (wi ph (wn ps)) $;

--| A negated syllogism deduction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2005.)
theorem nsyld (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ta ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ta)) $;

--| A negated syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1994.)
theorem nsyli (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th (wn ps)) $;

--| A negated syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-1996.)
theorem nsyl4 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ch $ >
  $ wi (wn ch) ps $;

--| A negated syllogism inference. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 20-May-2024.)
theorem nsyl5 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ch $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) ch $;

--| Theorem *3.2 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111, expressed with primitive connectives (see ~ pm3.2 ). (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Josh Purinton, 29-Dec-2000.)
theorem pm3_2im (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn (wi ph (wn ps)))) $;

--| Deduction joining the consequents of two premises. A deduction associated with ~ pm3.2im . (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-1992.)
theorem jc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wi ps (wn ch))) $;

--| Theorem joining the consequents of two premises. Theorem 8 of [Margaris] p. 60. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Josh Purinton, 29-Dec-2000.)
theorem jcn (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) (wn (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Deduction joining the consequents of two premises. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Apr-2024.)
theorem jcnd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wi ps ch)) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jul-2013.)
theorem impi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) ch $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 28-Nov-2008.)
theorem expi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $;

--| Simplification. Similar to Theorem *3.27 (Simp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 112. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Nov-2012.)
theorem simprim (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) ps $;

--| Simplification. Similar to Theorem *3.26 (Simp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 112. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Jul-2012.)
theorem simplim (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) ph $;

--| General instance of Theorem *2.5 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 9-Oct-2012.)
theorem pm2_5g (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi (wn ph) ch) $;

--| Theorem *2.5 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_5 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi (wn ph) ps) $;

--| Contrapositive of ~ ax-1 . (Contributed by BJ, 28-Oct-2023.)
theorem conax1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wn ps) $;

--| Weakening of ~ conax1 . General instance of ~ pm2.51 and of ~ pm2.52 . (Contributed by BJ, 28-Oct-2023.)
theorem conax1k (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi ch (wn ps)) $;

--| Theorem *2.51 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_51 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wn ps)) $;

--| Theorem *2.52 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Oct-2012.)
theorem pm2_52 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi (wn ph) (wn ps)) $;

--| A general instance of Theorem *2.521 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by BJ, 28-Oct-2023.)
theorem pm2_521g (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi ps ch) $;

--| A general instance of Theorem *2.521 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Oct-2012.)
theorem pm2_521g2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi ch ph) $;

--| Theorem *2.521 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. Instance of ~ pm2.521g and of ~ pm2.521g2 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_521 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi ps ph) $;

--| Exportation theorem ~ pm3.3 (closed form of ~ ex ) expressed with primitive connectives. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-1992.)
theorem expt (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) ch) (wi ph (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Importation theorem ~ pm3.1 (closed form of ~ imp ) expressed with primitive connectives. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jul-2013.)
theorem impt (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) ch) $;

--| Deduction eliminating an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 27-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Sep-2013.)
theorem pm2_61d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Inference eliminating an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-2005.)
theorem pm2_61d1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Inference eliminating an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.)
theorem pm2_61d2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Inference eliminating an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2023.)
theorem pm2_61i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Inference eliminating two antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Josh Purinton, 29-Dec-2000.)
theorem pm2_61ii (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi (wn ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ ch $;

--| Inference eliminating two antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 13-Jul-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Nov-2012.)
theorem pm2_61nii (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ch $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) ch $ >
  $ ch $;

--| Inference eliminating three antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Sep-2013.)
theorem pm2_61iii (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi (wn ps) (wi (wn ch) th)) $ >
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ps th $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ th $;

--| Inference joining the antecedents of two premises. For partial converses, see ~ jarri and ~ jarli . (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 19-Feb-2008.)
theorem ja (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) ch $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi (wi ph ps) ch $;

--| Deduction form of ~ ja . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 17-Sep-2011.)
theorem jad (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ps ch) th) $;

--| Weak Clavius law. If a formula implies its negation, then it is false. A form of "reductio ad absurdum", which can be used in proofs by contradiction. Theorem *2.01 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 100. Provable in minimal calculus, contrary to the Clavius law ~ pm2.18 . (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 21-Nov-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 31-Oct-2012.)
theorem pm2_01 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wn ph)) (wn ph) $;

--| Deduction based on reductio ad absurdum. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Mar-2013.)
theorem pm2_01d (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Theorem *2.6 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_6 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) ps) (wi (wi ph ps) ps) $;

--| Theorem *2.61 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. Useful for eliminating an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Sep-2013.)
theorem pm2_61 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi (wn ph) ps) ps) $;

--| Theorem *2.65 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. Proof by contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Mar-2013.)
theorem pm2_65 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ph (wn ps)) (wn ph)) $;

--| Inference for proof by contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2013.)
theorem pm2_65i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| A contradiction implies anything. Deduction from ~ pm2.21 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Jul-2019.)
theorem pm2_21dd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Deduction for proof by contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jun-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-May-2013.)
theorem pm2_65d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| The rule of modus tollens. The rule says, "if ` ps ` is not true, and ` ph ` implies ` ps ` , then ` ph ` must also be not true". Modus tollens is short for "modus tollendo tollens", a Latin phrase that means "the mode that by denying denies" - remark in [Sanford] p. 39. It is also called denying the consequent. Modus tollens is closely related to modus ponens ~ ax-mp . Note that this rule is also valid in intuitionistic logic. Inference associated with ~ con3i . (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2013.)
theorem mto (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| Modus tollens deduction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2013.)
theorem mtod (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Modus tollens inference. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jul-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Sep-2012.)
theorem mtoi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wn ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| A rule similar to modus tollens. Inference associated with ~ con2i . (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Sep-2013.)
theorem mt2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| A rule similar to modus tollens. Inference associated with ~ con1i . (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2013.)
theorem mt3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Peirce's axiom. A non-intuitionistic implication-only statement. Added to intuitionistic (implicational) propositional calculus, it gives classical (implicational) propositional calculus. For another non-intuitionistic positive statement, see ~ curryax . When ` F. ` is substituted for ` ps ` , then this becomes the Clavius law ~ pm2.18 . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 9-Oct-2012.)
theorem peirce (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ph) ph $;

--| The Inversion Axiom of the infinite-valued sentential logic (L-infinity) of Lukasiewicz. Using ~ dfor2 , we can see that this essentially expresses "disjunction commutes". Theorem *2.69 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. It is a special instance of the axiom "Roll", see ~ peirceroll . (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-2004.)
theorem looinv (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ps) (wi (wi ps ph) ph) $;

--| A self-implication (see ~ id ) does not imply its own negation. The justification theorem ~ bijust is one of its instances. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Josh Purinton, 29-Dec-2000.) Extract ~ bijust0 from proof of ~ bijust . (Revised by BJ, 19-Mar-2020.)
theorem bijust0 (ph: wff):
  $ wn (wi (wi ph ph) (wn (wi ph ph))) $;

--| Theorem used to justify the definition of the biconditional ~ df-bi . Instance of ~ bijust0 . (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.)
theorem bijust (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wi (wi (wn (wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wi ps ph)))) (wn (wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wi ps ph))))) (wn (wi (wn (wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wi ps ph)))) (wn (wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wi ps ph))))))) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include the biconditional connective.
term wb (ph ps: wff): wff;

--| Define the biconditional (logical "iff" or "if and only if"), also called biimplication. Definition ~ df-bi in this section is our first definition, which introduces and defines the biconditional connective ` <-> ` . We define a wff of the form ` ( ph <-> ps ) ` as an abbreviation for ` -. ( ( ph -> ps ) -> -. ( ps -> ph ) ) ` . Unlike most traditional developments, we have chosen not to have a separate symbol such as "Df." to mean "is defined as". Instead, we will later use the biconditional connective for this purpose ( ~ df-or is its first use), as it allows to use logic to manipulate definitions directly. This greatly simplifies many proofs since it eliminates the need for a separate mechanism for introducing and eliminating definitions. Of course, we cannot use this mechanism to define the biconditional itself, since it hasn't been introduced yet. Instead, we use a more general form of definition, described as follows. In its most general form, a definition is simply an assertion that introduces a new symbol (or a new combination of existing symbols, as in ~ df-3an ) that is eliminable and does not strengthen the existing language. The latter requirement means that the set of provable statements not containing the new symbol (or new combination) should remain exactly the same after the definition is introduced. Our definition of the biconditional may look unusual compared to most definitions, but it strictly satisfies these requirements. The justification for our definition is that if we mechanically replace ` ( ph <-> ps ) ` (the definiendum i.e. the thing being defined) with ` -. ( ( ph -> ps ) -> -. ( ps -> ph ) ) ` (the definiens i.e. the defining expression) in the definition, the definition becomes the previously proved theorem ~ bijust . It is impossible to use ~ df-bi to prove any statement expressed in the original language that can't be proved from the original axioms, because if we simply replace each instance of ~ df-bi in the proof with the corresponding ~ bijust instance, we will end up with a proof from the original axioms. Note that from Metamath's point of view, a definition is just another axiom - i.e. an assertion we claim to be true - but from our high level point of view, we are not strengthening the language. To indicate this fact, we prefix definition labels with "df-" instead of "ax-". (This prefixing is an informal convention that means nothing to the Metamath proof verifier; it is just a naming convention for human readability.) After we define the constant true ` T. ` ( ~ df-tru ) and the constant false ` F. ` ( ~ df-fal ), we will be able to prove these truth table values: ` ( ( T. <-> T. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ trubitru ), ` ( ( T. <-> F. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ trubifal ), ` ( ( F. <-> T. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ falbitru ), and ` ( ( F. <-> F. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ falbifal ). See ~ dfbi1 , ~ dfbi2 , and ~ dfbi3 for theorems suggesting typical textbook definitions of ` <-> ` , showing that our definition has the properties we expect. Theorem ~ dfbi1 is particularly useful if we want to eliminate ` <-> ` from an expression to convert it to primitives. Theorem ~ dfbi shows this definition rewritten in an abbreviated form after conjunction is introduced, for easier understanding. Contrast with ` \/ ` ( ~ df-or ), ` -> ` ( ~ wi ), ` -/\ ` ( ~ df-nan ), and ` \/_ ` ( ~ df-xor ). In some sense ` <-> ` returns true if two truth values are equal; ` = ` ( ~ df-cleq ) returns true if two classes are equal. (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-1992.)
axiom df_bi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wi (wi (wb ph ps) (wn (wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wi ps ph))))) (wn (wi (wn (wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wi ps ph)))) (wb ph ps)))) $;

--| Property of the biconditional connective. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.)
theorem impbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ph) (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Infer an equivalence from an implication and its converse. Inference associated with ~ impbi . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.)
theorem impbii (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ph $ >
  $ wb ph ps $;

--| Deduce an equivalence from two implications. Double deduction associated with ~ impbi and ~ impbii . Deduction associated with ~ impbid . (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 12-Oct-2010.)
theorem impbidd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi th ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ch th)) $;

--| Deduce an equivalence from two implications. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 12-May-2013.)
theorem impbid21d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ps (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ch th)) $;

--| Deduce an equivalence from two implications. Deduction associated with ~ impbi and ~ impbii . (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-1993.) Revised to prove it from ~ impbid21d . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 3-Nov-2012.)
theorem impbid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Relate the biconditional connective to primitive connectives. See ~ dfbi1ALT for an unusual version proved directly from axioms. (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.)
theorem dfbi1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wn (wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wi ps ph)))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ dfbi1 . This proof, discovered by Gregory Bush on 8-Mar-2004, has several curious properties. First, it has only 17 steps directly from the axioms and ~ df-bi , compared to over 800 steps were the proof of ~ dfbi1 expanded into axioms. Second, step 2 demands only the property of "true"; any axiom (or theorem) could be used. It might be thought, therefore, that it is in some sense redundant, but in fact no proof is shorter than this (measured by number of steps). Third, it illustrates how intermediate steps can "blow up" in size even in short proofs. Fourth, the compressed proof is only 182 bytes (or 17 bytes in D-proof notation), but the generated web page is over 200kB with intermediate steps that are essentially incomprehensible to humans (other than Gregory Bush). If there were an obfuscated code contest for proofs, this would be a contender. This "blowing up" and incomprehensibility of the intermediate steps vividly demonstrate the advantages of using many layered intermediate theorems, since each theorem is easier to understand. (Contributed by Gregory Bush, 10-Mar-2004.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem dfbi1ALT (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wn (wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wi ps ph)))) $;

--| Property of the biconditional connective. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.)
theorem biimp (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Infer an implication from a logical equivalence. Inference associated with ~ biimp . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.)
theorem biimpi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a biconditional and an implication. Useful for substituting an antecedent with a definition. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem sylbi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from an implication and a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem sylib (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by BJ, 30-Mar-2019.)
theorem sylbb (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Property of the biconditional connective. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Nov-2012.)
theorem biimpr (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wi ps ph) $;

--| Commutative law for the biconditional. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 10-Nov-2012.)
theorem bicom1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb ps ph) $;

--| Commutative law for the biconditional. Theorem *4.21 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.)
theorem bicom (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wb ps ph) $;

--| Commute two sides of a biconditional in a deduction. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem bicomd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch ps) $;

--| Inference from commutative law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem bicomi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ps ph $;

--| Infer an equivalence from two implications. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-2007.)
theorem impbid1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Infer an equivalence from two implications. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2013.)
theorem impbid2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| A variation on ~ impbid with contraposition. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 3-Jul-2009.)
theorem impcon4bid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Infer a converse implication from a logical equivalence. Inference associated with ~ biimpr . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2013.)
theorem biimpri (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ph $;

--| Deduce an implication from a logical equivalence. Deduction associated with ~ biimp and ~ biimpi . (Contributed by NM, 11-Jan-1993.)
theorem biimpd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $;

--| An inference from a biconditional, related to modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.)
theorem mpbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ ps $;

--| An inference from a biconditional, related to modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-1992.)
theorem mpbir (ph ps: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ ph $;

--| A deduction from a biconditional, related to modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem mpbid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| An inference from a nested biconditional, related to modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Oct-2012.)
theorem mpbii (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from an implication and a biconditional. Useful for substituting a consequent with a definition. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem sylibr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a biconditional and an implication. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem sylbir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from two biconditionals. Note on the various syllogism-like statements in set.mm. The hypothetical syllogism ~ syl infers an implication from two implications (and there are ~ 3syl and ~ 4syl for chaining more inferences). There are four inferences inferring an implication from one implication and one biconditional: ~ sylbi , ~ sylib , ~ sylbir , ~ sylibr ; four inferences inferring an implication from two biconditionals: ~ sylbb , ~ sylbbr , ~ sylbb1 , ~ sylbb2 ; four inferences inferring a biconditional from two biconditionals: ~ bitri , ~ bitr2i , ~ bitr3i , ~ bitr4i (and more for chaining more biconditionals). There are also closed forms and deduction versions of these, like, among many others, ~ syld , ~ syl5 , ~ syl6 , ~ mpbid , ~ bitrd , ~ bitrid , ~ bitrdi and variants. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Apr-2019.)
theorem sylbbr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wi ch ph $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Apr-2019.)
theorem sylbb1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ph ch $ >
  $ wi ps ch $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Apr-2019.)
theorem sylbb2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| A syllogism deduction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1994.)
theorem sylibd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A syllogism deduction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1994.)
theorem sylbid (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A deduction from a biconditional, related to modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1994.)
theorem mpbidi (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi th (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ph ch) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a nested implication and a biconditional. Useful for substituting an embedded antecedent with a definition. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jan-1993.)
theorem biimtrid (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph th) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a nested implication and a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem biimtrrid (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph th) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a nested implication and a biconditional. This is in the process of being renamed to ~ biimtrrid . (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem syl5bir (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph th) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jan-1993.)
theorem syl5ib (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph th) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jun-2007.)
theorem syl5ibcom (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1994.)
theorem syl5ibr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph ps) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jun-2007.)
theorem syl5ibrcom (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch ps) $;

--| Deduce a converse implication from a logical equivalence. Deduction associated with ~ biimpr and ~ biimpri . (Contributed by NM, 11-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Sep-2013.)
theorem biimprd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch ps) $;

--| Deduce a commuted implication from a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Sep-2013.)
theorem biimpcd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ph ch) $;

--| Deduce a converse commuted implication from a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Dec-2013.)
theorem biimprcd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph ps) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a nested implication and a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem syl6ib (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a nested implication and a biconditional. Useful for substituting an embedded consequent with a definition. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.)
theorem syl6ibr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wb th ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-1994.)
theorem syl6bi (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1994.)
theorem syl6bir (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ch ps) $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a doubly nested implication and a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem syl7bi (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wi th (wi ps ta)) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi th (wi ph ta)) $;

--| A syllogism rule of inference. The second premise is used to replace the consequent of the first premise. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1994.)
theorem syl8ib (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wb th ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ta)) $;

--| A deduction from a biconditional, related to modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem mpbird (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| An inference from a nested biconditional, related to modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Oct-2012.)
theorem mpbiri (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| A syllogism deduction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1994.)
theorem sylibrd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A syllogism deduction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1994.)
theorem sylbird (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ch ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| Principle of identity for logical equivalence. Theorem *4.2 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117. This is part of Frege's eighth axiom per Proposition 54 of [Frege1879] p. 50; see also ~ eqid . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.)
theorem biid (ph: wff):
  $ wb ph ph $;

--| Principle of identity with antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-1995.)
theorem biidd (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ps) $;

--| Two propositions are equivalent if they are both true. Closed form of ~ 2th . Equivalent to a ~ biimp -like version of the xor-connective. This theorem stays true, no matter how you permute its operands. This is evident from its sharper version ` ( ph <-> ( ps <-> ( ph <-> ps ) ) ) ` . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 12-May-2013.)
theorem pm5_1im (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Two truths are equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.)
theorem _2th (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wb ph ps $;

--| Two truths are equivalent. Deduction form. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jun-2012.)
theorem _2thd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Two self-implications (see ~ id ) are equivalent. This theorem, rather trivial in our axiomatization, is (the biconditional form of) a standard axiom for monothetic BCI logic. This is the most general theorem of which ~ trujust is an instance. Relatedly, this would be the justification theorem if the definition of ` T. ` were ~ dftru2 . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Sep-2022.)
theorem monothetic (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ph) (wi ps ps) $;

--| Inference that converts a biconditional implied by one of its arguments, into an implication. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2003.)
theorem ibi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Inference that converts a biconditional implied by one of its arguments, into an implication. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jul-2004.)
theorem ibir (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ph) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Deduction that converts a biconditional implied by one of its arguments, into an implication. Deduction associated with ~ ibi . (Contributed by NM, 26-Jun-2004.)
theorem ibd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional. Theorem *5.74 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2013.)
theorem pm5_74 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wb ps ch)) (wb (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional (inference form). (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1994.)
theorem pm5_74i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional (reverse inference form). (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1994.)
theorem pm5_74ri (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 21-Mar-1996.)
theorem pm5_74d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wi ps ch) (wi ps th)) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 19-Mar-1997.)
theorem pm5_74rd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb (wi ps ch) (wi ps th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ch th)) $;

--| An inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Oct-2012.)
theorem bitri (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wb ph ch $;

--| An inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem bitr2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wb ch ph $;

--| An inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.)
theorem bitr3i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wb ph ch $;

--| An inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem bitr4i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch ps $ >
  $ wb ph ch $;

--| Deduction form of ~ bitri . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Apr-2013.)
theorem bitrd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps th) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ bitr2i . (Contributed by NM, 9-Jun-2004.)
theorem bitr2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ps) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ bitr3i . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem bitr3d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch th) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ bitr4i . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1993.)
theorem bitr4d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps th) $;

--| A syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem bitrid (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ph th) $;

--| A syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1993.)
theorem bitr2id (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ch (wb th ph) $;

--| A syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem bitr3id (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ph th) $;

--| A syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-1994.)
theorem bitr3di (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb ps th $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch th) $;

--| A syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem bitrdi (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps th) $;

--| A syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem bitr2di (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ps) $;

--| A syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem bitr4di (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb th ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps th) $;

--| A syllogism inference from two biconditionals. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-1994.)
theorem bitr4id (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps th) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference, useful for removing a definition from both sides of an implication. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1994.)
theorem _3imtr3i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wb ph ch $ >
  $ wb ps th $ >
  $ wi ch th $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference, useful for applying a definition to both sides of an implication. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem _3imtr4i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch ph $ >
  $ wb th ps $ >
  $ wi ch th $;

--| More general version of ~ 3imtr3i . Useful for converting conditional definitions in a formula. (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-1996.)
theorem _3imtr3d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $;

--| More general version of ~ 3imtr4i . Useful for converting conditional definitions in a formula. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-1995.)
theorem _3imtr4d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ta ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $;

--| More general version of ~ 3imtr3i . Useful for converting definitions in a formula. (Contributed by NM, 20-May-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Dec-2013.)
theorem _3imtr3g (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wb ps th $ >
  $ wb ch ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $;

--| More general version of ~ 3imtr4i . Useful for converting definitions in a formula. (Contributed by NM, 20-May-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Dec-2013.)
theorem _3imtr4g (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wb th ps $ >
  $ wb ta ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $;

--| A chained inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem _3bitri (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb ph th $;

--| A chained inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3bitrri (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb th ph $;

--| A chained inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3bitr2i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb ph th $;

--| A chained inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3bitr2ri (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb th ph $;

--| A chained inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.)
theorem _3bitr3i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ph ch $ >
  $ wb ps th $ >
  $ wb ch th $;

--| A chained inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem _3bitr3ri (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ph ch $ >
  $ wb ps th $ >
  $ wb th ch $;

--| A chained inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. This inference is frequently used to apply a definition to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem _3bitr4i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch ph $ >
  $ wb th ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $;

--| A chained inference from transitive law for logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.)
theorem _3bitr4ri (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch ph $ >
  $ wb th ps $ >
  $ wb th ch $;

--| Deduction from transitivity of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 13-Aug-1999.)
theorem _3bitrd (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ta) $;

--| Deduction from transitivity of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3bitrrd (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ta ps) $;

--| Deduction from transitivity of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3bitr2d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ta) $;

--| Deduction from transitivity of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3bitr2rd (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ta ps) $;

--| Deduction from transitivity of biconditional. Useful for converting conditional definitions in a formula. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-1996.)
theorem _3bitr3d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $;

--| Deduction from transitivity of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3bitr3rd (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ta th) $;

--| Deduction from transitivity of biconditional. Useful for converting conditional definitions in a formula. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1995.)
theorem _3bitr4d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ta ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $;

--| Deduction from transitivity of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3bitr4rd (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ta ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ta th) $;

--| More general version of ~ 3bitr3i . Useful for converting definitions in a formula. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-1995.)
theorem _3bitr3g (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb ps th $ >
  $ wb ch ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $;

--| More general version of ~ 3bitr4i . Useful for converting definitions in a formula. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.)
theorem _3bitr4g (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb th ps $ >
  $ wb ta ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $;

--| Double negation. Theorem *4.13 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem notnotb (ph: wff):
  $ wb ph (wn (wn ph)) $;

--| A biconditional form of contraposition. Theorem *4.1 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 116. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.)
theorem con34b (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wi (wn ps) (wn ph)) $;

--| A contraposition deduction. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1994.)
theorem con4bid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb (wn ps) (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Deduction negating both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1994.)
theorem notbid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wn ps) (wn ch)) $;

--| Contraposition. Theorem *4.11 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem notbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wb (wn ph) (wn ps)) $;

--| Negate both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-May-2013.)
theorem notbii (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wn ph) (wn ps) $;

--| A contraposition inference. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1994.)
theorem con4bii (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb ph ps $;

--| An inference from a biconditional, related to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Oct-2012.)
theorem mtbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wn ps $;

--| An inference from a biconditional, related to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Oct-2012.)
theorem mtbir (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| A deduction from a biconditional, similar to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 26-Nov-1995.)
theorem mtbid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| A deduction from a biconditional, similar to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1994.)
theorem mtbird (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| An inference from a biconditional, similar to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 27-Nov-1995.)
theorem mtbii (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| An inference from a biconditional, similar to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-1995.)
theorem mtbiri (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wn ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from an implication and a biconditional. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Dec-2013.)
theorem sylnib (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from an implication and a biconditional. Useful for substituting a consequent with a definition. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Dec-2013.)
theorem sylnibr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb ch ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a biconditional and an implication. Useful for substituting an antecedent with a definition. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Dec-2013.)
theorem sylnbi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ch $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a biconditional and an implication. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Dec-2013.)
theorem sylnbir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ch $;

--| Replacement of a subexpression by an equivalent one. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2014.)
theorem xchnxbi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ wb ph ch $ >
  $ wb (wn ch) ps $;

--| Replacement of a subexpression by an equivalent one. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2014.)
theorem xchnxbir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ wb ch ph $ >
  $ wb (wn ch) ps $;

--| Replacement of a subexpression by an equivalent one. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2014.)
theorem xchbinx (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wb ph (wn ch) $;

--| Replacement of a subexpression by an equivalent one. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2014.)
theorem xchbinxr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb ch ps $ >
  $ wb ph (wn ch) $;

--| Introduce an antecedent to both sides of a logical equivalence. This and the next three rules are useful for building up wff's around a definition, in order to make use of the definition. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Feb-2013.)
theorem imbi2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wi ch ph) (wi ch ps) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ jcnd as of 10-Apr-2024. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem jcndOLD (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Inference adding a biconditional to the left in an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-May-2013.)
theorem bibi2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wb ch ph) (wb ch ps) $;

--| Inference adding a biconditional to the right in an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem bibi1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wb ph ch) (wb ps ch) $;

--| The equivalence of two equivalences. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem bibi12i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb (wb ph ch) (wb ps th) $;

--| Deduction adding an antecedent to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.)
theorem imbi2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wi th ps) (wi th ch)) $;

--| Deduction adding a consequent to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Sep-2013.)
theorem imbi1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wi ps th) (wi ch th)) $;

--| Deduction adding a biconditional to the left in an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-May-2013.)
theorem bibi2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wb th ps) (wb th ch)) $;

--| Deduction adding a biconditional to the right in an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.)
theorem bibi1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wb ps th) (wb ch th)) $;

--| Deduction joining two equivalences to form equivalence of implications. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1993.)
theorem imbi12d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wi ps th) (wi ch ta)) $;

--| Deduction joining two equivalences to form equivalence of biconditionals. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem bibi12d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wb ps th) (wb ch ta)) $;

--| Closed form of ~ imbi12i . Was automatically derived from its "Virtual Deduction" version and the Metamath program "MM-PA> MINIMIZE__WITH *" command. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 18-Mar-2012.)
theorem imbi12 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wi (wb ch th) (wb (wi ph ch) (wi ps th))) $;

--| Theorem *4.84 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem imbi1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wi ph ch) (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Theorem *4.85 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-May-2013.)
theorem imbi2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wi ch ph) (wi ch ps)) $;

--| Introduce a consequent to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Sep-2013.)
theorem imbi1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wi ph ch) (wi ps ch) $;

--| Join two logical equivalences to form equivalence of implications. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1993.)
theorem imbi12i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb (wi ph ch) (wi ps th) $;

--| Theorem *4.86 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem bibi1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wb ph ch) (wb ps ch)) $;

--| Closed nested implication form of ~ bitr3i . Derived automatically from ~ bitr3VD . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.)
theorem bitr3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wi (wb ph ch) (wb ps ch)) $;

--| Contraposition. Theorem *4.12 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 15-Apr-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem con2bi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph (wn ps)) (wb ps (wn ph)) $;

--| A contraposition deduction. (Contributed by NM, 15-Apr-1995.)
theorem con2bid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch (wn ps)) $;

--| A contraposition deduction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-1999.)
theorem con1bid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb (wn ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wn ch) ps) $;

--| A contraposition inference. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Oct-2012.)
theorem con1bii (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ wb (wn ps) ph $;

--| A contraposition inference. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem con2bii (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb ps (wn ph) $;

--| Contraposition. Bidirectional version of ~ con1 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem con1b (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wn ph) ps) (wi (wn ps) ph) $;

--| Contraposition. Bidirectional version of ~ con2 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem con2b (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wn ps)) (wi ps (wn ph)) $;

--| A wff is equivalent to itself with true antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-1996.)
theorem biimt (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Theorem *5.5 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm5_5 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb (wi ph ps) ps) $;

--| Inference introducing a theorem as an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Nov-2012.)
theorem a1bi (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb ps (wi ph ps) $;

--| A false consequent falsifies an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Nov-2012.)
theorem mt2bi (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb (wn ps) (wi ps (wn ph)) $;

--| Modus-tollens-like theorem. (Contributed by NM, 7-Apr-2001.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Nov-2012.)
theorem mtt (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wb (wn ps) (wi ps ph)) $;

--| If a proposition is false, then implying it is equivalent to being false. One of four theorems that can be used to simplify an implication ` ( ph -> ps ) ` , the other ones being ~ ax-1 (true consequent), ~ pm2.21 (false antecedent), ~ pm5.5 (true antecedent). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-May-2019.)
theorem imnot (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ps) (wb (wi ph ps) (wn ph)) $;

--| Theorem *5.501 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm5_501 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Implication in terms of implication and biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Jan-2013.)
theorem ibib (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wi ph (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Implication in terms of implication and biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 29-Apr-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Dec-2013.)
theorem ibibr (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wi ph (wb ps ph)) $;

--| A wff is equivalent to its equivalence with a truth. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem tbt (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb ps (wb ps ph) $;

--| The negation of a wff is equivalent to the wff's equivalence to falsehood. (Contributed by Juha Arpiainen, 19-Jan-2006.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jan-2013.)
theorem nbn2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wb (wn ps) (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Transfer negation via an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jan-2013.)
theorem bibif (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ps) (wb (wb ph ps) (wn ph)) $;

--| The negation of a wff is equivalent to the wff's equivalence to falsehood. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Oct-2013.)
theorem nbn (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wb (wn ps) (wb ps ph) $;

--| Transfer falsehood via equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 11-Sep-2006.)
theorem nbn3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb (wn ps) (wb ps (wn ph)) $;

--| Two propositions are equivalent if they are both false. Closed form of ~ 2false . Equivalent to a ~ biimpr -like version of the xor-connective. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 13-May-2013.)
theorem pm5_21im (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi (wn ps) (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Two falsehoods are equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-May-2013.)
theorem _2false (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wb ph ps $;

--| Two falsehoods are equivalent (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2013.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2024.)
theorem _2falsed (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ 2falsed as of 11-Apr-2024. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2013.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2falsedOLD (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Two propositions implying a false one are equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 16-Feb-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-May-2013.)
theorem pm5_21ni (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wb ph ch) $;

--| Eliminate an antecedent implied by each side of a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1999.)
theorem pm5_21nii (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch ps $ >
  $ wi ps (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wb ph ch $;

--| Eliminate an antecedent implied by each side of a biconditional, deduction version. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Nov-2012.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Oct-2013.)
theorem pm5_21ndd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ch ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch th) $;

--| Combine antecedents into a single biconditional. This inference, reminiscent of ~ ja , is reversible: The hypotheses can be deduced from the conclusion alone (see ~ pm5.1im and ~ pm5.21im ). (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 13-May-2013.)
theorem bija (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi (wn ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wb ph ps) ch $;

--| Theorem *5.18 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 124. This theorem says that logical equivalence is the same as negated "exclusive or". (Contributed by NM, 28-Jun-2002.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 20-Jun-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Oct-2013.)
theorem pm5_18 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wn (wb ph (wn ps))) $;

--| Two ways to express "exclusive or". (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2006.)
theorem xor3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wn (wb ph ps)) (wb ph (wn ps)) $;

--| Move negation outside of biconditional. Compare Theorem *5.18 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jun-2002.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Sep-2013.)
theorem nbbn (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb (wn ph) ps) (wn (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Associative law for the biconditional. An axiom of system DS in Vladimir Lifschitz, "On calculational proofs", Annals of Pure and Applied Logic, 113:207-224, 2002, ~ http://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/ai-lab/pub-view.php?PubID=26805 . Interestingly, this law was not included in _Principia Mathematica_ but was apparently first noted by Jan Lukasiewicz circa 1923. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Juha Arpiainen, 19-Jan-2006.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Sep-2013.)
theorem biass (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wb (wb ph ps) ch) (wb ph (wb ps ch)) $;

--| Lukasiewicz's shortest axiom for equivalential calculus. Storrs McCall, ed., _Polish Logic 1920-1939_ (Oxford, 1967), p. 96. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-2005.)
theorem biluk (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wb (wb ch ps) (wb ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *5.19 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm5_19 (ph: wff):
  $ wn (wb ph (wn ph)) $;

--| Logical equivalence of commuted antecedents. Part of Theorem *4.87 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.)
theorem bi2_04 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ps (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Antecedent absorption implication. Theorem *5.4 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem pm5_4 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wi ph ps)) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Distributive law for implication. Compare Theorem *5.41 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem imdi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *5.41 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Oct-2012.)
theorem pm5_41 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) (wi ph (wi ps ch)) $;

--| The antecedent of one side of a biconditional can be moved out of the biconditional to become the antecedent of the remaining biconditional. (Contributed by BJ, 1-Jan-2025.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Jan-2025.)
theorem imbibi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb (wi ph ps) ch) (wi ph (wb ps ch)) $;

--| Theorem *4.8 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_8 (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wn ph)) (wn ph) $;

--| A formula is equivalent to its negation implying it. Theorem *4.81 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122. Note that the second step, using ~ pm2.24 , could also use ~ ax-1 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_81 (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wn ph) ph) ph $;

--| Simplify an implication between two implications when the antecedent of the first is a consequence of the antecedent of the second. The reverse form is useful in producing the successor step in induction proofs. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Sep-2013.)
theorem imim21b (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ps ph) (wb (wi (wi ph ch) (wi ps th)) (wi ps (wi ch th))) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include conjunction ("and").
term wa (ph ps: wff): wff;

--| Define conjunction (logical "and"). Definition of [Margaris] p. 49. When both the left and right operand are true, the result is true; when either is false, the result is false. For example, it is true that ` ( 2 = 2 /\ 3 = 3 ) ` . After we define the constant true ` T. ` ( ~ df-tru ) and the constant false ` F. ` ( ~ df-fal ), we will be able to prove these truth table values: ` ( ( T. /\ T. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ truantru ), ` ( ( T. /\ F. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ truanfal ), ` ( ( F. /\ T. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ falantru ), and ` ( ( F. /\ F. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ falanfal ). This is our first use of the biconditional connective in a definition; we use the biconditional connective in place of the traditional "<=def=>", which means the same thing, except that we can manipulate the biconditional connective directly in proofs rather than having to rely on an informal definition substitution rule. Note that if we mechanically substitute ` -. ( ph -> -. ps ) ` for ` ( ph /\ ps ) ` , we end up with an instance of previously proved theorem ~ biid . This is the justification for the definition, along with the fact that it introduces a new symbol ` /\ ` . Contrast with ` \/ ` ( ~ df-or ), ` -> ` ( ~ wi ), ` -/\ ` ( ~ df-nan ), and ` \/_ ` ( ~ df-xor ). (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-1993.)
axiom df_an (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph ps) (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) $;

--| Theorem *4.63 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_63 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) (wa ph ps) $;

--| Theorem *4.67 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_67 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wn (wi (wn ph) (wn ps))) (wa (wn ph) ps) $;

--| Express an implication in terms of a negated conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1994.)
theorem imnan (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wn ps)) (wn (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Infer an implication from a negated conjunction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Sep-2015.)
theorem imnani (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wa ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Implication in terms of conjunction and negation. Theorem 3.4(27) of [Stoll] p. 176. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Oct-2012.)
theorem iman (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wn (wa ph (wn ps))) $;

--| Law of noncontradiction. Theorem *3.24 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111 (who call it the "law of contradiction"). (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem pm3_24 (ph: wff):
  $ wn (wa ph (wn ph)) $;

--| Express a conjunction in terms of a negated implication. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1994.)
theorem annim (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wn ps)) (wn (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Theorem *4.61 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_61 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wn (wi ph ps)) (wa ph (wn ps)) $;

--| Theorem *4.65 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_65 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wn (wi (wn ph) ps)) (wa (wn ph) (wn ps)) $;

--| Importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Eric Schmidt, 22-Dec-2006.)
theorem imp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Importation inference with commuted antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 25-May-2005.)
theorem impcom (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ph) ch $;

--| Variant of ~ con3d with importation. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem con3dimp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn ch)) (wn ps) $;

--| Eliminate the right side of a negated conjunction in an implication. (Contributed by ML, 17-Oct-2020.)
theorem mpnanrd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wa ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| Importation deduction. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1994.)
theorem impd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $;

--| Importation deduction with commuted antecedents. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 24-Sep-2022.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Oct-2022.)
theorem impcomd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ch ps) th) $;

--| Exportation inference. (This theorem used to be labeled "exp" but was changed to "ex" so as not to conflict with the math token "exp", per the June 2006 Metamath spec change.) A translation of natural deduction rule ` -> ` I ( ` -> ` introduction), see ~ natded . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Eric Schmidt, 22-Dec-2006.)
theorem ex (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $;

--| Exportation inference with commuted antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 25-May-2005.)
theorem expcom (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ph ch) $;

--| Commuted form of ~ expd . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 18-Mar-2012.) Shorten ~ expd . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jul-2022.)
theorem expdcom (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch (wi ph th)) $;

--| Exportation deduction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jul-2022.)
theorem expd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ expcom . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 22-Jul-2012.)
theorem expcomd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wi ps th)) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem imp31 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem imp32 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem exp31 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem exp32 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.) Shorten ~ imp4a . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jul-2021.)
theorem imp4b (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi (wa ch th) ta) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jul-2021.)
theorem imp4a (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi (wa ch th) ta)) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem imp4c (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wa ps ch) th) ta) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem imp4d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps (wa ch th)) ta) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem imp41 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ta $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem imp42 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th) ta $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem imp43 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) ta $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem imp44 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa ps ch) th)) ta $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem imp45 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps (wa ch th))) ta $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2012.) Shorten ~ exp4a . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem exp4b (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi (wa ch th) ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem exp4a (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi (wa ch th) ta)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem exp4c (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wa ps ch) th) ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem exp4d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps (wa ch th)) ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem exp41 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem exp42 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th) ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem exp43 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem exp44 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa ps ch) th)) ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1994.)
theorem exp45 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps (wa ch th))) ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th ta))) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Jul-2009.)
theorem imp5d (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) (wi (wa th ta) et) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Aug-2022.)
theorem imp5a (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi (wa th ta) et))) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Jul-2009.)
theorem imp5g (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi (wa (wa ch th) ta) et) $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Jul-2009.)
theorem imp55 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wa ps (wa ch th))) ta) et $;

--| An importation inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Jul-2009.)
theorem imp511 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa ps (wa ch th)) ta)) et $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Jul-2009.)
theorem exp5c (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) (wi (wa th ta) et)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Jul-2009.)
theorem exp5j (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wa (wa ps ch) th) ta) et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Jul-2009.)
theorem exp5l (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wa ps ch) (wa th ta)) et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 30-Aug-2009.)
theorem exp53 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) ta) et $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)))) $;

--| Theorem *3.3 (Exp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 112. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Mar-2013.)
theorem pm3_3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wa ph ps) ch) (wi ph (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Theorem *3.31 (Imp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 112. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Mar-2013.)
theorem pm3_31 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wa ph ps) ch) $;

--| Import-export theorem. Part of Theorem *4.87 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Mar-2013.)
theorem impexp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wa ph ps) ch) (wi ph (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Mixed importation/commutation inference. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jun-2013.)
theorem impancom (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) (wi ps th) $;

--| A deduction version of exportation, followed by importation. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2008.)
theorem expdimp (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi ch th) $;

--| Exportation followed by a deduction version of importation. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2008.)
theorem expimpd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $;

--| Import a wff into a right conjunct. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 30-Aug-2009.)
theorem impr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $;

--| Export a wff from a left conjunct. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem impl (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $;

--| Export a wff from a right conjunct. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 30-Aug-2009.)
theorem expr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi ch th) $;

--| Export a wff from a left conjunct. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Aug-2009.)
theorem expl (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $;

--| Inference commuting conjunction in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem ancoms (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ph) ch $;

--| Theorem *3.22 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Nov-2012.)
theorem pm3_22 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wa ps ph) $;

--| Commutative law for conjunction. Theorem *4.3 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jun-1998.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Nov-2012.)
theorem ancom (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph ps) (wa ps ph) $;

--| Commutation of conjuncts in consequent. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 14-Aug-2009.)
theorem ancomd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ch ps) $;

--| Commuting conjunction in a biconditional. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 17-Jun-2018.)
theorem biancomi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ch ps) $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $;

--| Commuting conjunction in a biconditional, deduction form. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 3-Oct-2018.)
theorem biancomd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa th ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $;

--| Closed form of ~ ancoms . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.)
theorem ancomst (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wa ph ps) ch) (wi (wa ps ph) ch) $;

--| Deduction commuting conjunction in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.)
theorem ancomsd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ch ps) th) $;

--| Associative law for conjunction applied to antecedent (eliminates syllogism). (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2002.)
theorem anasss (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $;

--| Associative law for conjunction applied to antecedent (eliminates syllogism). (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2002.)
theorem anassrs (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $;

--| Associative law for conjunction. Theorem *4.32 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem anass (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ch) (wa ph (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Join antecedents with conjunction ("conjunction introduction"). Theorem *3.2 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111. Its associated inference is ~ pm3.2i and its associated deduction is ~ jca (and the double deduction is ~ jcad ). See ~ pm3.2im for a version using only implication and negation. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Nov-2012.)
theorem pm3_2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Infer conjunction of premises. Inference associated with ~ pm3.2 . Its associated deduction is ~ jca (and the double deduction is ~ jcad ). (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem pm3_2i (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wa ph ps $;

--| Join antecedents with conjunction. Theorem *3.21 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem pm3_21 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa ps ph)) $;

--| Nested conjunction of antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-1993.)
theorem pm3_43i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ph ch) (wi ph (wa ps ch))) $;

--| Theorem *3.43 (Comp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 113. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm3_43 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) (wi ph (wa ps ch)) $;

--| A theorem similar to the standard definition of the biconditional. Definition of [Margaris] p. 49. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-1993.)
theorem dfbi2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ps ph)) $;

--| Definition ~ df-bi rewritten in an abbreviated form to help intuitive understanding of that definition. Note that it is a conjunction of two implications; one which asserts properties that follow from the biconditional and one which asserts properties that imply the biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-2008.)
theorem dfbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wa (wi (wb ph ps) (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ps ph))) (wi (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ps ph)) (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Importation inference from a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1994.)
theorem biimpa (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Importation inference from a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1994.)
theorem biimpar (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) ps $;

--| Importation inference from a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1994.)
theorem biimpac (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ph) ch $;

--| Importation inference from a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1994.)
theorem biimparc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ch ph) ps $;

--| Inference adding a conjunct to the right of an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1993.)
theorem adantr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) ps $;

--| Inference adding a conjunct to the left of an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2012.)
theorem adantl (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ch ph) ps $;

--| Elimination of a conjunct. Theorem *3.26 (Simp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 112. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Jun-2022.)
theorem simpl (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ph $;

--| Inference eliminating a conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-1994.)
theorem simpli (ph ps: wff):
  $ wa ph ps $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Elimination of a conjunct. Theorem *3.27 (Simp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 112. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Jun-2022.)
theorem simpr (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ps $;

--| Inference eliminating a conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-1994.)
theorem simpri (ph ps: wff):
  $ wa ph ps $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Introduction of conjunct inside of a contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1993.)
theorem intnan (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wn (wa ps ph) $;

--| Introduction of conjunct inside of a contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1995.)
theorem intnanr (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wn (wa ph ps) $;

--| Introduction of conjunct inside of a contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jul-2005.)
theorem intnand (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wa ch ps)) $;

--| Introduction of conjunct inside of a contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jul-2005.)
theorem intnanrd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to the left of an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 4-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Dec-2012.)
theorem adantld (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa th ps) ch) $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to the right of an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 4-May-1994.)
theorem adantrd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps th) ch) $;

--| Theorem *3.41 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 113. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm3_41 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ch) (wi (wa ph ps) ch) $;

--| Theorem *3.42 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 113. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm3_42 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ps ch) (wi (wa ph ps) ch) $;

--| Deduction eliminating a conjunct. A translation of natural deduction rule ` /\ ` EL ( ` /\ ` elimination left), see ~ natded . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem simpld (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Deduction eliminating a conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) A translation of natural deduction rule ` /\ ` ER ( ` /\ ` elimination right), see ~ natded . (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Oct-2013.)
theorem simprd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Deduction eliminating a conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1998.)
theorem simprbi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Deduction eliminating a conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1998.)
theorem simplbi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Deduction eliminating a conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2007.)
theorem simprbda (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Deduction eliminating a conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2007.)
theorem simplbda (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $;

--| Deduction eliminating a conjunct. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.)
theorem simplbi2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ch ph) $;

--| Closed form of ~ simplbi2com . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 22-Jul-2012.)
theorem simplbi2comt (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph (wa ps ch)) (wi ch (wi ps ph)) $;

--| A deduction eliminating a conjunct, similar to ~ simplbi2 . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 22-Jul-2012.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Nov-2012.)
theorem simplbi2com (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ps ph) $;

--| Implication from an eliminated conjunct implied by the antecedent. (Contributed by BJ/AV, 5-Apr-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Mar-2022.)
theorem simpl2im (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Implication from an eliminated conjunct equivalent to the antecedent. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Mar-2022.)
theorem simplbiim (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| An inference for implication elimination. (Contributed by Giovanni Mascellani, 23-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem impel (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) ch $;

--| Modus ponens conjoining dissimilar antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2008.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.)
theorem mpan9 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $;

--| Nested syllogism inference conjoining dissimilar antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.)
theorem sylan9 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ch ta) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wi ps ta) $;

--| Nested syllogism inference conjoining dissimilar antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem sylan9r (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ch ta) $ >
  $ wi (wa th ph) (wi ps ta) $;

--| Nested syllogism inference conjoining dissimilar antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 4-Mar-1995.)
theorem sylan9bb (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wb ch ta) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wb ps ta) $;

--| Nested syllogism inference conjoining dissimilar antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 4-Mar-1995.)
theorem sylan9bbr (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wb ch ta) $ >
  $ wi (wa th ph) (wb ps ta) $;

--| Deduce conjunction of the consequents of two implications ("join consequents with 'and'"). Deduction form of ~ pm3.2 and ~ pm3.2i . Its associated deduction is ~ jcad . Equivalent to the natural deduction rule ` /\ ` I ( ` /\ ` introduction), see ~ natded . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Oct-2012.)
theorem jca (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ps ch) $;

--| Deduction conjoining the consequents of two implications. Deduction form of ~ jca and double deduction form of ~ pm3.2 and ~ pm3.2i . (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Jul-2013.)
theorem jcad (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa ch th)) $;

--| Inference conjoining the consequents of two implications. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 12-Oct-2010.)
theorem jca2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ps th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa ch th)) $;

--| Join three consequents. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Aug-2009.)
theorem jca31 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wa ps ch) th) $;

--| Join three consequents. (Contributed by FL, 1-Aug-2009.)
theorem jca32 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ps (wa ch th)) $;

--| Deduction replacing implication with conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-1993.)
theorem jcai (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ps ch) $;

--| Distributive law for implication over conjunction. Compare Theorem *4.76 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Nov-2013.)
theorem jcab (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wa ps ch)) (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *4.76 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_76 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) (wi ph (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Inference conjoining a theorem to left of consequent in an implication. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-1993.)
theorem jctil (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ch ps) $;

--| Inference conjoining a theorem to right of consequent in an implication. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-1993.)
theorem jctir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ps ch) $;

--| Inference conjoining a consequent of a consequent to the right of the consequent in an implication. See also ~ ex-natded5.3i . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.) (Revised by AV, 20-Aug-2019.)
theorem jccir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ps ch) $;

--| Inference conjoining a consequent of a consequent to the left of the consequent in an implication. Remark: One can also prove this theorem using ~ syl and ~ jca (as done in ~ jccir ), which would be 4 bytes shorter, but one step longer than the current proof. (Proof modification is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 20-Aug-2019.)
theorem jccil (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ch ps) $;

--| Inference conjoining a theorem to the left of a consequent. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Oct-2012.)
theorem jctl (ph ps: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ps ph) $;

--| Inference conjoining a theorem to the right of a consequent. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Oct-2012.)
theorem jctr (ph ps: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ph ps) $;

--| Deduction conjoining a theorem to left of consequent in an implication. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-2005.)
theorem jctild (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa th ch)) $;

--| Deduction conjoining a theorem to right of consequent in an implication. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-2005.)
theorem jctird (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa ch th)) $;

--| Introduction of antecedent as conjunct. Theorem *4.73 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 30-Mar-1994.)
theorem iba (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ps ph)) $;

--| Introduction of antecedent as conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-1995.)
theorem ibar (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ph ps)) $;

--| A wff is equivalent to its conjunction with truth. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem biantru (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb ps (wa ps ph) $;

--| A wff is equivalent to its conjunction with truth. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1994.)
theorem biantrur (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb ps (wa ph ps) $;

--| A wff is equivalent to its conjunction with truth. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Oct-2013.)
theorem biantrud (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch (wa ch ps)) $;

--| A wff is equivalent to its conjunction with truth. (Contributed by NM, 1-May-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.)
theorem biantrurd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch (wa ps ch)) $;

--| A wff conjoined with falsehood is false. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Nov-2012.)
theorem bianfi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ps ph) $;

--| A wff conjoined with falsehood is false. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem bianfd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1999.)
theorem baib (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ps (wb ph ch) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jul-1994.)
theorem baibr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ps (wb ch ph) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jan-2020.)
theorem rbaibr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ps ph) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jan-2020.)
theorem rbaib (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ph ps) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem baibd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) (wb ps th) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem rbaibd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wb ps ch) $;

--| Absorb a hypothesis into the second member of a biconditional. (Contributed by FL, 15-Feb-2007.)
theorem bianabs (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ph ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Theorem *5.44 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm5_44 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wb (wi ph ch) (wi ph (wa ps ch))) $;

--| Theorem *5.42 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm5_42 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ph (wi ps (wa ph ch))) $;

--| Conjoin antecedent to left of consequent. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1994.)
theorem ancl (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Conjoin antecedent to left of consequent. Theorem *4.7 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-1999.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Mar-2013.)
theorem anclb (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wi ph (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Conjoin antecedent to right of consequent. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1994.)
theorem ancr (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph (wa ps ph)) $;

--| Conjoin antecedent to right of consequent. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-1999.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Mar-2013.)
theorem ancrb (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wi ph (wa ps ph)) $;

--| Deduction conjoining antecedent to left of consequent. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.)
theorem ancli (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ph ps) $;

--| Deduction conjoining antecedent to right of consequent. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1994.)
theorem ancri (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wa ps ph) $;

--| Deduction conjoining antecedent to left of consequent in nested implication. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Nov-2012.)
theorem ancld (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Deduction conjoining antecedent to right of consequent in nested implication. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Nov-2012.)
theorem ancrd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa ch ps)) $;

--| Importation with conjunction in consequent. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1994.)
theorem impac (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wa ch ps) $;

--| Conjoin antecedent to left of consequent in nested implication. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Jul-2013.)
theorem anc2l (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ph (wi ps (wa ph ch))) $;

--| Conjoin antecedent to right of consequent in nested implication. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1994.)
theorem anc2r (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ph (wi ps (wa ch ph))) $;

--| Deduction conjoining antecedent to left of consequent in nested implication. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Dec-2012.)
theorem anc2li (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Deduction conjoining antecedent to right of consequent in nested implication. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Dec-2012.)
theorem anc2ri (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa ch ph)) $;

--| Implication in terms of biconditional and conjunction. Theorem *4.71 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Dec-2012.)
theorem pm4_71 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wb ph (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Implication in terms of biconditional and conjunction. Theorem *4.71 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120 (with conjunct reversed). (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-1999.)
theorem pm4_71r (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wb ph (wa ps ph)) $;

--| Inference converting an implication to a biconditional with conjunction. Inference from Theorem *4.71 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2004.)
theorem pm4_71i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ph ps) $;

--| Inference converting an implication to a biconditional with conjunction. Inference from Theorem *4.71 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120 (with conjunct reversed). (Contributed by NM, 1-Dec-2003.)
theorem pm4_71ri (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ps ph) $;

--| Deduction converting an implication to a biconditional with conjunction. Deduction from Theorem *4.71 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem pm4_71d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Deduction converting an implication to a biconditional with conjunction. Deduction from Theorem *4.71 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-2005.)
theorem pm4_71rd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch ps)) $;

--| Theorem *4.24 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.)
theorem pm4_24 (ph: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ph ph) $;

--| Idempotent law for conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2004.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Mar-2014.)
theorem anidm (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph ph) ph $;

--| Conjunction idempotence with antecedent. (Contributed by Roy F. Longton, 8-Aug-2005.)
theorem anidmdbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wa ps ps)) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Inference from idempotent law for conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-1994.)
theorem anidms (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ph) ps $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Distribution of implication with conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Dec-2012.)
theorem imdistan (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Distribution of implication with conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1994.)
theorem imdistani (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch) $;

--| Distribution of implication with conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2002.)
theorem imdistanri (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ph) (wa ch ph) $;

--| Distribution of implication with conjunction (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2004.)
theorem imdistand (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) (wa ps th)) $;

--| Distribution of implication with conjunction (deduction version with conjoined antecedent). (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2011.)
theorem imdistanda (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) (wa ps th)) $;

--| Theorem *5.3 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.)
theorem pm5_3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wa ph ps) ch) (wi (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional. Theorem *5.32 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1994.)
theorem pm5_32 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wb ps ch)) (wb (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional (inference form). (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1994.)
theorem pm5_32i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional (inference form). (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1995.)
theorem pm5_32ri (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb (wa ps ph) (wa ch ph) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1996.)
theorem pm5_32d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa ps ch) (wa ps th)) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 25-Dec-2004.)
theorem pm5_32rd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa ch ps) (wa th ps)) $;

--| Distribution of implication over biconditional (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 9-Dec-2006.)
theorem pm5_32da (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa ps ch) (wa ps th)) $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Nov-2012.)
theorem sylan (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1994.)
theorem sylanb (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1994.)
theorem sylanbr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $;

--| Syllogism inference. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem sylanbrc (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wb th (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Syllogism inference combined with contraction. (Contributed by NM, 16-Mar-2012.)
theorem syl2anc (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Double syllogism inference combined with contraction. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 29-Sep-2023.)
theorem syl2anc2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Syllogism inference combined with modus ponens. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem sylancl (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Syllogism inference combined with modus ponens. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem sylancr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Syllogism inference with commutation of antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jul-2008.)
theorem sylancom (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ch ps) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $;

--| Syllogism inference combined with a biconditional. (Contributed by BJ, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem sylanblc (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wb (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Syllogism inference combined with a biconditional. (Contributed by BJ, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem sylanblrc (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wb th (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| A syllogism deduction with conjoined antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Apr-2013.)
theorem syldan (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $;

--| A syllogism deduction. (Contributed by SN, 16-Jul-2024.)
theorem sylbida (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Nov-2012.)
theorem sylan2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ph) th $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem sylan2b (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ph) th $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem sylan2br (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ch ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ph) th $;

--| A double syllogism inference. For an implication-only version, see ~ syl2im . (Contributed by NM, 31-Jan-1997.)
theorem syl2an (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ta ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ta) th $;

--| A double syllogism inference. For an implication-only version, see ~ syl2imc . (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2013.)
theorem syl2anr (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ta ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ta ph) th $;

--| A double syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-1999.)
theorem syl2anb (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ta ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ta) th $;

--| A double syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-1999.)
theorem syl2anbr (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wb ch ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ta) th $;

--| A syllogism inference combined with contraction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2004.)
theorem sylancb (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| A syllogism inference combined with contraction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2004.)
theorem sylancbr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wb ch ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| A syllogism deduction with conjoined antecedents. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 20-Jun-2011.)
theorem syldanl (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) th) ta $;

--| A syllogism deduction. (Contributed by NM, 15-Dec-2004.)
theorem syland (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ch th) ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps th) ta) $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 2-May-1996.)
theorem sylani (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ps (wi (wa ch th) ta) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi (wa ph th) ta) $;

--| A syllogism deduction. (Contributed by NM, 15-Dec-2004.)
theorem sylan2d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa th ch) ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa th ps) ta) $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1994.)
theorem sylan2i (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ps (wi (wa ch th) ta) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi (wa ch ph) ta) $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1999.)
theorem syl2ani (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi et th $ >
  $ wi ps (wi (wa ch th) ta) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi (wa ph et) ta) $;

--| A syllogism deduction. (Contributed by NM, 15-Dec-2004.)
theorem syl2and (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ch ta) et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps th) et) $;

--| Conjoin antecedents and consequents in a deduction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Dec-2013.)
theorem anim12d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps th) (wa ch ta)) $;

--| Variant of ~ anim12d where the second implication does not depend on the antecedent. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 12-Oct-2010.)
theorem anim12d1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps th) (wa ch ta)) $;

--| Add a conjunct to right of antecedent and consequent in a deduction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1994.)
theorem anim1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps th) (wa ch th)) $;

--| Add a conjunct to left of antecedent and consequent in a deduction. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem anim2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa th ps) (wa th ch)) $;

--| Conjoin antecedents and consequents of two premises. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Dec-2013.)
theorem anim12i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) (wa ps th) $;

--| Variant of ~ anim12i with commutation. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem anim12ci (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) (wa th ps) $;

--| Introduce conjunct to both sides of an implication. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem anim1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch) $;

--| Introduce conjunct to both sides of an implication. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 24-Sep-2022.)
theorem anim1ci (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) (wa ch ps) $;

--| Introduce conjunct to both sides of an implication. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem anim2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ch ph) (wa ch ps) $;

--| Conjoin antecedents and consequents in a deduction. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jul-2013.)
theorem anim12ii (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ps ta) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wi ps (wa ch ta)) $;

--| Conjoin antecedents and consequents in a deduction. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 16-Jun-2011.)
theorem anim12dan (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps th)) (wa ch ta) $;

--| Deduction joining nested implications to form implication of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Feb-1996.)
theorem im2anan9 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wi (wa ps ta) (wa ch et)) $;

--| Deduction joining nested implications to form implication of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Feb-1996.)
theorem im2anan9r (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa th ph) (wi (wa ps ta) (wa ch et)) $;

--| Theorem *3.45 (Fact) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 113. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm3_45 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Introduce a left conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem anbi2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wa ch ph) (wa ch ps) $;

--| Introduce a right conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem anbi1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch) $;

--| Variant of ~ anbi2i with commutation. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jun-2011.)
theorem anbi2ci (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wa ph ch) (wa ch ps) $;

--| Variant of ~ anbi1i with commutation. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem anbi1ci (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wa ch ph) (wa ps ch) $;

--| Conjoin both sides of two equivalences. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem anbi12i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb (wa ph ch) (wa ps th) $;

--| Variant of ~ anbi12i with commutation. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem anbi12ci (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb (wa ph ch) (wa th ps) $;

--| Deduction adding a left conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem anbi2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa th ps) (wa th ch)) $;

--| Deduction adding a right conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem anbi1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa ps th) (wa ch th)) $;

--| Deduction joining two equivalences to form equivalence of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem anbi12d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa ps th) (wa ch ta)) $;

--| Introduce a right conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. Theorem *4.36 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem anbi1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Introduce a left conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem anbi2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wa ch ph) (wa ch ps)) $;

--| Introduce a proposition as left conjunct on the left-hand side and right conjunct on the right-hand side of an equivalence. Deduction form. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 22-May-2021.)
theorem anbi1cd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa th ps) (wa ch th)) $;

--| Double commutation in conjunction. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 27-Jun-2019.)
theorem an2anr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa (wa ps ph) (wa th ch)) $;

--| Theorem *4.38 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_38 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wb ph ch) (wb ps th)) (wb (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) $;

--| Deduction joining two equivalences to form equivalence of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1995.)
theorem bi2anan9 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wb (wa ps ta) (wa ch et)) $;

--| Deduction joining two equivalences to form equivalence of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 19-Feb-1996.)
theorem bi2anan9r (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa th ph) (wb (wa ps ta) (wa ch et)) $;

--| Deduction joining two biconditionals with different antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2004.)
theorem bi2bian9 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wb (wb ps ta) (wb ch et)) $;

--| An inference to merge two lists of conjuncts. (Contributed by Giovanni Mascellani, 23-May-2019.)
theorem bianass (ph ps ch et: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wb (wa et ph) (wa (wa et ps) ch) $;

--| An inference to merge two lists of conjuncts. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 24-Sep-2022.)
theorem bianassc (ph ps ch et: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wb (wa et ph) (wa (wa ps et) ch) $;

--| Swap two conjuncts. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 18-Sep-2022.)
theorem an21 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ch) (wa ps (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Swap two conjuncts. Note that the first digit (1) in the label refers to the outer conjunct position, and the next digit (2) to the inner conjunct position. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 18-Sep-2022.)
theorem an12 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wa ps ch)) (wa ps (wa ph ch)) $;

--| A rearrangement of conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Dec-2012.)
theorem an32 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ch) (wa (wa ph ch) ps) $;

--| A rearrangement of conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jun-2012.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 31-Dec-2012.)
theorem an13 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wa ps ch)) (wa ch (wa ps ph)) $;

--| A rearrangement of conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jun-2012.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 31-Dec-2012.)
theorem an31 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ch) (wa (wa ch ps) ph) $;

--| Swap two conjuncts in antecedent. The label suffix "s" means that ~ an12 is combined with ~ syl (or a variant). (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.)
theorem an12s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wa ph ch)) th $;

--| Inference commuting a nested conjunction in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 24-May-2006.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem ancom2s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ch ps)) th $;

--| Swap two conjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-2006.)
theorem an13s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ch (wa ps ph)) th $;

--| Swap two conjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.)
theorem an32s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) ps) th $;

--| Inference commuting a nested conjunction in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 24-May-2006.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem ancom1s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ps ph) ch) th $;

--| Swap two conjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-2006.)
theorem an31s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ch ps) ph) th $;

--| Commutative-associative law for conjunction in an antecedent. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2011.)
theorem anass1rs (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) ps) th $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jul-1994.)
theorem an4 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa (wa ph ch) (wa ps th)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-1996.)
theorem an42 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa (wa ph ch) (wa th ps)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 conjuncts. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 24-Sep-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 29-Jun-2011.)
theorem an43 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa (wa ph th) (wa ps ch)) $;

--| A rearrangement of conjuncts. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 25-Sep-2010.)
theorem an3 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa ph th) $;

--| Inference rearranging 4 conjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1995.)
theorem an4s (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) (wa ps th)) ta $;

--| Inference rearranging 4 conjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1995.)
theorem an42s (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) (wa th ps)) ta $;

--| Absorption into embedded conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabs1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ph) (wa ph ps) $;

--| Absorption into embedded conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 9-Dec-2012.)
theorem anabs5 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wa ph ps)) (wa ph ps) $;

--| Absorption into embedded conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabs7 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ps (wa ph ps)) (wa ph ps) $;

--| Absorption of antecedent with conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 24-Mar-1996.)
theorem anabsan (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ph) ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 31-Dec-2012.)
theorem anabss1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ph) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.)
theorem anabss4 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ps ph) ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Jan-2013.)
theorem anabss5 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ph ps)) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jun-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabsi5 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ph ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2000.)
theorem anabsi6 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ph) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabsi7 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ps (wi (wa ph ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-1999.)
theorem anabsi8 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ps (wi (wa ps ph) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabss7 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ps (wa ph ps)) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent with conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-2004.)
theorem anabsan2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ps)) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Jan-2013.)
theorem anabss3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Distribution of conjunction over conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1995.)
theorem anandi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wa ps ch)) (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Distribution of conjunction over conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-1995.)
theorem anandir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ch) (wa (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Inference that undistributes conjunction in the antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-2004.)
theorem anandis (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) ta $;

--| Inference that undistributes conjunction in the antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-2004.)
theorem anandirs (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) ta $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2005.)
theorem sylanl1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ps ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) th) ta $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2005.)
theorem sylanl2 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ps ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ps ph) th) ta $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2005.)
theorem sylanr1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wa ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wa ph th)) ta $;

--| A syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2005.)
theorem sylanr2 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wa ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wa ch ph)) ta $;

--| A syllogism deduction combined with conjoining antecedents. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 28-Oct-2011.)
theorem syl6an (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps th) ta $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch ta) $;

--| ~ syl2anr with antecedents in standard conjunction form. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 27-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Mar-2022.)
theorem syl2an2r (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) ta $;

--| ~ syl2an with antecedents in standard conjunction form. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 27-Aug-2016.)
theorem syl2an2 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ch ph) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ch ph) ta $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 23-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Nov-2012.)
theorem mpdan (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens with commutation of antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-2003.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Apr-2013.)
theorem mpancom (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ps ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ps ch $;

--| A deduction which "stacks" a hypothesis. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Mar-2021.)
theorem mpidan (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Apr-2013.)
theorem mpan (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ps ch $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2012.)
theorem mpan2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 13-Apr-1995.)
theorem mp2an (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ ch $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.)
theorem mp4an (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ th $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) ta $ >
  $ ta $;

--| A deduction based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.)
theorem mpan2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| A deduction based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Apr-2013.)
theorem mpand (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2012.)
theorem mpani (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2012.)
theorem mpan2i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.)
theorem mp2ani (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| A deduction based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.)
theorem mp2and (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 16-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Apr-2013.)
theorem mpanl1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 16-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.)
theorem mpanl2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 13-Jul-2005.)
theorem mpanl12 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi ch th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.)
theorem mpanr1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Apr-2013.)
theorem mpanr2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jul-2009.)
theorem mpanr12 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 30-Dec-2004.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Apr-2013.)
theorem mpanlr1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) th) ta $;

--| Detach truth from conjunction in biconditional. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem mpbirand (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps th) $;

--| Detach truth from conjunction in biconditional. Deduction form. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 24-Sep-2022.)
theorem mpbiran2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Detach truth from conjunction in biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-1996.)
theorem mpbiran (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wb ph ch $;

--| Detach truth from conjunction in biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.)
theorem mpbiran2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wb ph ps $;

--| Detach a conjunction of truths in a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-2005.)
theorem mpbir2an (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Detach a conjunction of truths in a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem mpbi2and (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Detach a conjunction of truths in a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem mpbir2and (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 4-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem adantll (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa th ph) ps) ch $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 4-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem adantlr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph th) ps) ch $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 4-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem adantrl (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa th ps)) ch $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 4-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem adantrr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps th)) ch $;

